We are migrating checkout-service from Spring Boot 2.7 to 3.2. We have custom converters and JSONB user types that were using org.hibernate.type.AbstractSingleColumnStandardBasicType which is removed in Hibernate 6. Here is our old PostgreSQL JSONB mapping in checkout-service/src/main/java/com/cymbal/checkout/model/OrderMetadataType.java:

```java
public class OrderMetadataType extends AbstractSingleColumnStandardBasicType<OrderMetadata> {
    public OrderMetadataType() {
        super(JsonBinaryTypeDescriptor.INSTANCE, new OrderMetadataDescriptor());
    }
}
```

Rewrite this to use Hibernate 6's `@Type` or `@JdbcTypeCode(SqlTypes.JSON)` annotation directly on the `Order.java` entity field, remove the obsolete user type class, and ensure JPA queries using metadata json containment operators still work.

Migrate the legacy event dispatcher in checkout-service from synchronized blocks to virtual-thread safe constructs. We noticed thread pinning under load because the dispatcher synchronizes on database connection callbacks:

```java
public class OrderEventDispatcher {
    private final Object lock = new Object();
    public void dispatch(OrderEvent event) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            // calls blocking network socket to PubSub / DB
            auditRepository.save(event.toAuditLog());
            pubsubTemplate.publish("orders-topic", event.payload());
        }
    }
}
```

Refactor this class to eliminate `synchronized` blocks so Java 21 carrier threads don't get pinned. Replace the monitor lock with `ReentrantLock` or restructure to lock-free concurrent queues with a dedicated virtual worker.

We are upgrading checkout-service from Java 17 to Java 21 for ticket CYM-3419. We have several old utility classes in com.cymbal.checkout.util that implement custom tuple objects (Pair, Triple, Quad) with manual equals/hashCode/toString. Here is an example in CartBucket.java:

```java
public class PriceTierPair {
    private final BigDecimal minAmount;
    private final BigDecimal discountPercent;
    // 40 lines of boilerplate constructors, equals, hashcode
}
```

Migrate all inner tuple classes in CartBucket.java and PricingUtils.java to compact local Java records. Furthermore, refactor the nested if-else chain in calculateTierDiscount() to use switch with guarded pattern matching (`when` clauses).

Help migrate storefront-web/pages/order-confirmation/[orderId].tsx to the App Router at app/order-confirmation/[orderId]/page.tsx as part of CYM-4120. Here is the current getServerSideProps implementation:

```tsx
export const getServerSideProps: GetServerSideProps = async (ctx) => {
  const { orderId } = ctx.params!;
  const res = await fetch(`${process.env.INTERNAL_CHECKOUT_URL}/orders/${orderId}`, {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${ctx.req.cookies['session_token']}` }
  });
  if (!res.ok) return { notFound: true };
  const order = await res.json();
  return { props: { order } };
};
```

Rewrite this as an async Server Component that uses `next/headers` cookies and calls the checkout API directly, sets up dynamic metadata via `generateMetadata`, and wraps the client-side order status tracking polling widget in a Suspense boundary with a skeleton fallback.

Complete the migration of storefront-web/pages/search.tsx to the App Router at app/search/page.tsx. The search page currently relies on useRouter query params, client-side re-renders, and an old Redux search slice:

```tsx
export default function SearchPage() {
  const router = useRouter();
  const { q, category, minPrice, maxPrice, sort } = router.query;
  const dispatch = useDispatch();
  useEffect(() => {
    if (q) dispatch(fetchSearchResults({ q, category, minPrice, maxPrice, sort }));
  }, [q, category, minPrice, maxPrice, sort]);
  // ...
}
```

Refactor this page so the search parameters are read server-side via `searchParams` prop in `app/search/page.tsx`, stream results with React Suspense, and update URL search parameters client-side using `useSearchParams` and `useRouter.replace` with shallow transitions.

We are upgrading merchant-portal to Angular 18 for CYM-3550. We need to migrate the entire transaction dispute resolution module from NgModule-based routing to standalone component lazy loading. In merchant-portal/src/app/disputes/disputes-routing.module.ts:

```typescript
const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: '',
    component: DisputesListComponent,
    children: [
      { path: ':id', loadChildren: () => import('./detail/detail.module').then(m => m.DetailModule) }
    ]
  }
];
```

Refactor this route tree to use `loadComponent: () => import('./detail/detail.component').then(m => m.DetailComponent)` with standalone components, eliminate `DisputesModule` and `DetailModule`, and update the route guard to use functional `canActivateFn` instead of class-based `CanActivate`.

Help refactor our custom table pagination and virtual scrolling in merchant-portal/src/app/settlements/settlements.component.ts. The component currently uses CDK Virtual Scroll with manual ngOnChanges change detection and ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck():

```typescript
export class SettlementsComponent implements OnChanges {
  @Input() transactions: Transaction[] = [];
  ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges) {
    if (changes['transactions']) {
      this.dataSource.data = this.transactions;
      this.cdr.markForCheck();
    }
  }
}
```

Convert this component to use `input()` and `output()` signal transforms introduced in Angular 17.2/18, replace manual change detection with signal effects (`effect()`), and update the template `@for` loop to use `track item.transactionId`.

We need to extract a storage abstraction in checkout-service for CYM-3604. Right now, OrderInvoiceService directly injects Google Cloud Storage `com.google.cloud.storage.Storage` across multiple methods:

```java
public class OrderInvoiceService {
    @Autowired private Storage gcsStorage;
    public String uploadPdf(String orderId, byte[] pdfBytes) {
        BlobId blobId = BlobId.of("cymbal-invoices", orderId + ".pdf");
        BlobInfo info = BlobInfo.newBuilder(blobId).setContentType("application/pdf").build();
        gcsStorage.create(info, pdfBytes);
        return "https://storage.googleapis.com/cymbal-invoices/" + orderId + ".pdf";
    }
}
```

Extract an `InvoiceStorageProvider` interface with methods `storeInvoice(String orderId, byte[] data)` and `fetchInvoiceUrl(String orderId)`. Implement `GcsInvoiceStorageProvider` as the primary bean and provide a `LocalDiskInvoiceStorageProvider` for offline dev profiles.

In payments-gateway, we are supporting multiple payment aggregators (Razorpay, Pine Labs, Juspay) for UPI and card checkouts. Currently, `payments-gateway/internal/service/payment.go` contains switch statements checking `req.GatewayProvider` and calling provider-specific structs directly:

```go
switch req.Provider {
case "razorpay":
    return s.razorpayClient.Initiate(ctx, req)
case "juspay":
    return s.juspayClient.CreateOrder(ctx, req)
}
```

Refactor this by extracting a unified `PaymentGatewayAdapter` interface with `InitiatePayment`, `VerifySignature`, and `QueryStatus` methods. Move each aggregator's implementation into `internal/gateway/razorpay/` and `internal/gateway/juspay/`, and use a registry/factory pattern to resolve adapters at runtime.

Help migrate the checkout payment polling in cymbal-android from RxJava interval polling to Kotlin Flow for CYM-3715. Here is our old polling implementation in PaymentPollingManager.kt:

```kotlin
class PaymentPollingManager(private val api: CheckoutApi) {
    fun pollPaymentStatus(orderId: String): Observable<PaymentStatus> {
        return Observable.interval(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
            .flatMapSingle { api.getPaymentStatus(orderId) }
            .takeUntil { it.isTerminal() }
            .subscribeOn(Schedulers.io())
            .observeOn(AndroidSchedulers.mainThread())
    }
}
```

Refactor this to return a `Flow<PaymentStatus>` using `flow { ... }` with `delay(2000)` and `transformWhile { ... }`. Ensure cancellation is cooperative when the user navigates away from the checkout screen, and bind it to the view lifecycle using `repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED)`.

We are migrating cymbal-ios network layer from legacy callback closures to Swift structured concurrency for CYM-3844. Here is our current API client method in `NetworkClient.swift`:

```swift
func executeRequest<T: Decodable>(route: ApiRoute, completion: @escaping (Result<T, ApiError>) -> Void) {
    urlSession.dataTask(with: route.urlRequest) { data, response, error in
        if let error = error {
            DispatchQueue.main.async { completion(.failure(.network(error))) }
            return
        }
        // 30 lines of status code checks and JSONDecoder
    }.resume()
}
```

Refactor this method to `func executeRequest<T: Decodable & Sendable>(route: ApiRoute) async throws -> T` using `URLSession.shared.data(for: request)`. Support cancellation propagation using `withTaskCancellationHandler` or checking `Task.isCancelled`, and ensure decoding happens off the main actor.

Port our parallel batch asset preloader in cymbal-ios/Catalog/CatalogAssetPreloader.swift to Swift TaskGroup. Currently, the preloader uses `DispatchGroup` and `OperationQueue` with maxConcurrentOperationCount = 4:

```swift
let group = DispatchGroup()
for url in assetUrls {
    group.enter()
    downloadQueue.addOperation {
        downloadAsset(url) { _ in group.leave() }
    }
}
group.notify(queue: .main) { onComplete() }
```

Refactor this to an async function `func preloadAssets(urls: [URL]) async -> [URL: UIImage]` using `withTaskGroup(of: (URL, UIImage?).self)`. Limit concurrency to at most 4 concurrent downloads, discard failed downloads cleanly without aborting the entire group, and conform the preloader class to `actor`.

We are migrating internal inter-service communication from HTTP/1.1 REST to gRPC for CYM-3920. In checkout-service, `PaymentGatewayRestClient.java` sends JSON POST requests to `http://payments-gateway:8080/api/v1/authorize`:

```java
public PaymentResponse authorize(PaymentRequest req) {
    HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
    headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
    headers.set("X-Idempotency-Key", req.getIdempotencyKey());
    HttpEntity<PaymentRequest> entity = new HttpEntity<>(req, headers);
    return restTemplate.postForObject(gatewayUrl + "/api/v1/authorize", entity, PaymentResponse.class);
}
```

Refactor this to use the generated `PaymentServiceGrpc.PaymentServiceBlockingStub`. Pass the idempotency key and correlation ID as gRPC metadata headers (`Metadata.Key`), configure exponential retry interceptor on UNAVAILABLE status, and delete the obsolete REST client and JSON request/response classes.

We are refactoring checkout-service to adopt Domain-Driven Design (DDD) aggregates for CYM-4800. Right now, `Order.java` entity directly mutates `OrderItem`, `PaymentRecord`, `ShippingAddress`, and `FulfillmentStatus` from external service classes without enforcing domain invariants:

```java
// OrderService.java
Order order = orderRepository.findById(orderId);
order.getItems().remove(item);
order.setStatus(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
orderRepository.save(order);
// external classes directly touching collections and internal status
```

Refactor `Order` into a true Aggregate Root:
1. Make all collections unmodifiable via `Collections.unmodifiableList()` in getters.
2. Encapsulate business operations in domain methods (`order.cancel(reason)`, `order.addItem(product, qty)`).
3. Enforce validation rules and domain invariants inside the aggregate before status transitions.

Migrate the order state machine in checkout-service from an ad-hoc status enum with switch statements to Spring StateMachine for CYM-4850. Currently, `OrderService.java` manually checks transitions:

```java
if (order.getStatus() == OrderStatus.PENDING && event == Event.PAYMENT_RECEIVED) {
    order.setStatus(OrderStatus.PAID);
} else if (order.getStatus() == OrderStatus.PAID && event == Event.FULFILL) {
    order.setStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);
} else {
    throw new InvalidOrderStateException("Illegal transition");
}
```

Refactor this logic into a declarative `StateMachineConfigurerAdapter<OrderStatus, OrderEvent>`: configure transitions with guards (`hasSufficientInventoryGuard`) and action handlers (`notifyMerchantAction`), and persist the state machine context into PostgreSQL using Spring StateMachine JPA repository.
